1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,920
Claiming got cheap.

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:03,340
Checking did not.

3
00:00:04,540 --> 00:00:09,460
Here is a reference: two authors, a year, a title, a journal and some page numbers.

4
00:00:10,060 --> 00:00:12,260
It looks like every other reference you have read.

5
00:00:12,580 --> 00:00:14,140
I made it up for this video.

6
00:00:14,740 --> 00:00:20,180
A chatbot can write one like it in under a second, and nothing on the page tells you
whether the work exists.

7
00:00:20,780 --> 00:00:23,260
To find out, someone has to go and look for it.

8
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:29,940
In 2023, William Walters and Esther Wilder had ChatGPT write eighty-four short
literature reviews, and then they searched for every one of the six hundred and

9
00:00:29,940 --> 00:00:35,420
thirty-six references.

10
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:40,920
Fifty-five percent of the references from GPT-3.5 did not exist.

11
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:44,080
For GPT-4, the share was eighteen percent.

12
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:52,400
Many of the real ones were wrong in their details too: forty-three percent for
GPT-3.5 and twenty-four percent for GPT-4.

13
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,680
On the page, the invented and the real look the same.

14
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:57,740
Only the search told them apart.

15
00:00:58,940 --> 00:01:01,020
Science already pays people to check.

16
00:01:01,340 --> 00:01:06,540
By reviewers' own reports, one peer review takes about six hours of a skilled
person's time.

17
00:01:07,140 --> 00:01:11,940
Balazs Aczel and colleagues estimated about twenty-two million reviews in 2020.

18
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:17,760
Six hours each comes to over a hundred million hours: about fifteen thousand years
of work, spent in one year.

19
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:23,760
It is an estimate built from approximate rates, and the authors expect the true
figure to be higher.

20
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:27,220
Meanwhile, the unchecked claim does not wait.

21
00:01:27,540 --> 00:01:37,700
Soroush Vosoughi, Deb Roy and Sinan Aral followed about a hundred and twenty-six
thousand rumor cascades on Twitter, from 2006 to 2017.

22
00:01:38,300 --> 00:01:44,860
False stories were seventy percent more likely to be retweeted, and the truth took
about six times as long to reach fifteen hundred people.

23
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:50,040
Bots sped up true and false news at the same rate, which points to people as the
difference.

24
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:52,960
That study measures spread, not cost.

25
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:57,820
The old line that refuting a claim takes ten times the effort of making it is an
adage.

26
00:01:58,140 --> 00:02:00,160
Nobody has measured that ratio.

27
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:07,060
Claims got cheap, and a check still costs hours, so the gap can only close from the
checking side.

28
00:02:07,660 --> 00:02:11,340
When the data travel with the claim, a check becomes a rerun.

29
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:17,020
Tom Hardwicke and colleagues tried to reproduce the numbers in thirty-five
psychology papers that shared their data.

30
00:02:17,620 --> 00:02:22,380
Where the numbers came out straight away, a check took about two to four person
hours, by their estimate.

31
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:27,960
Where they had to ask the authors for help, it took five to twenty-five, and weeks
of waiting.

32
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:33,020
Thirteen of the thirty-five still had at least one number nobody could reproduce.

33
00:02:33,340 --> 00:02:38,260
None of those misses clearly changed a paper's conclusion, and the rerun is how
anyone knows that.

34
00:02:39,460 --> 00:02:40,600
Two things to keep.

35
00:02:40,920 --> 00:02:43,800
How a claim looks tells you nothing about whether it holds.

36
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:48,280
And the cheapest check is the one that ships with the claim, so anyone can rerun it.

37
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:51,520
The voice you heard is a synthesized version of mine.

38
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:54,760
Every number on screen links to its source under the video.
